#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193647} :activity/id #uuid "02255835-1ac4-5d6f-b968-e337f6d42745" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193647 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193876 :tutorial/content "Recall that $H\\subseteq \\mathbb R^n$ is a **subspace** of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ if and only if it satisfies the following properties:\n\n- *Closure under vector addition:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are in $H,$ then their sum $\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v}$ is also in $H.$\n\n- *Closure under scalar multiplication:* \n\n$\\qquad$ If $\\mathbf{u}$ is in $H,$ then $c\\cdot \\mathbf{u}$ is also in $H$ for any scalar number $c.$\n\nIn the vector space $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ there are two **trivial subspaces**.\n\n- One trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH=\\{\\mathbf{0}\\}\n$$\n\n consisting of just the zero vector. This is a subspace because the sum of two zero vectors is the zero vector, and any multiple of the zero vector is the zero vector.\n\n- Another trivial subspace is the set \n\n$$\nH = \\mathbb{R}^n\n$$\n\n consisting of the entire set $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is a subspace because the sum of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ and any multiple of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "01ed4d92-df35-5f3c-aa41-43246f6ea9e9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6854 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "93a1b04d-d02e-5aae-bd94-6c2a571d80d7" :step/math-academy-id 41649 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193651}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193648 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192504 :question/id #uuid "32f7b0e6-5a68-52ab-9d86-ea71387cfb77" :question/math-academy-id "e-10975"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051046}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193312} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247149} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426532}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "f42bb3f4-1e7f-53f5-ad85-ca2e942d30b3" :step/math-academy-id 41650 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193649 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192781 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192505 :question/id #uuid "554fc7b1-62ca-5189-bda1-712f518ad617" :question/math-academy-id "e-10976"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1094661c-8c7c-5f85-b731-4dadb0aec310" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193313} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193318} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247150} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317380} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323702} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434595} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479092} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479117}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Using Set Notation Are Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "4c579fcb-af17-53b9-89c4-b7c4d86d6a8f" :step/math-academy-id 41653 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192782 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192506 :question/id #uuid "6e80df98-c67c-5f27-a44b-d0bc8f681ac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10979"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4a8dbbab-2dea-5fff-86f0-3411f5405ee9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193319} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193384} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426536}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "68866355-7603-545f-b26a-1a0cee48c958" :step/math-academy-id 41665 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193649}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193877 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ are straight lines that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows a subspace in $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n![](images/3c/3c0ea9584ae3229a7930c6ef2b53031770a7190b980b5b595cc233cae29ea49d.png)\n\nLet's now consider a few examples of subsets of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that are *not* subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/5a/5aac9808e9196653ee54974b495443a95d8913cd0b220a6893acb68ff3b967bb.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is *not* a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II is not a subspace because $\\mathbf u$ and $\\mathbf v$ belong to the set while $\\mathbf u+\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under vector addition.\n\n- Diagram III is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $-\\mathbf v$ does not. So the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :tutorial/id #uuid "33c42a7a-a5c2-5b36-a839-4fab6351c9e4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6857 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "18e8a405-fc8d-5344-9133-d42b106e46e6" :step/math-academy-id 41670 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193648}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193878 :tutorial/content "In $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n*The only non-trivial subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3$ are straight lines or planes that pass through the origin.*\n\nFor example, the diagram below shows two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/57/57b6625b607537d1c90e849d6ee6ac8480648aa4ad6e3a357bedc8d8408256ad.png)\n\nOn the other hand, the sets shown in the diagrams below are *not* subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n![](images/f4/f4fbaee302e1c25e740f4164841a674243a43197b0f20ddb35d2d41bb4175b68.png)\n\n- Diagram I shows a plane that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Diagram II shows a line that does not pass through the origin. It is not a subspace because $\\mathbf v$ belongs to the set while $2\\mathbf v$ does not. So, the set is not closed under scalar multiplication." :tutorial/id #uuid "8d9802f0-c392-5e37-afb4-72f73451813e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6861 :tutorial/title "Non-Trivial Subspaces in Three-Dimensional Space"} :step/id #uuid "40d83f58-34e5-5b7b-b300-54ae33e99a59" :step/math-academy-id 41676 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193879 :tutorial/content "Lastly, consider the set $\\mathbb{R},$ which belongs to the family of vector spaces $\\mathbb{R}^n$ (when $n=1$). This vector space has only two subspaces, both of which are trivial:\n\n- the zero subspace $\\{0\\},$ which contains only the number $0,$ and\n\n- the whole space $\\mathbb{R}$ (or, formally, $\\mathbb{R}^1$), which contains all the real numbers." :tutorial/id #uuid "fb0671eb-0c5d-59dc-a3f9-cda72b4da034" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6862 :tutorial/title "Final Notes About the Set of Real Numbers"} :step/id #uuid "fda08207-59ef-5481-816d-d4abed842b3a" :step/math-academy-id 41677}] :activity/title "Subspaces of N-Dimensional Space: Geometric Interpretation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192920}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434597} :activity/id #uuid "888de114-5cc0-5506-ab02-2950778f2cdd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048337 :topic/id #uuid "96874e3b-8b97-5c33-995d-d34a9d3daf21" :topic/math-academy-id 4077} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434596 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42472aa9-7d67-5ed4-b98a-d5c155583284" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474810} {:answer/id #uuid "50eed6d6-c97a-50b5-8b9a-6beba0cf0a59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474811}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474811} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca653d76-a75a-51a4-80dd-bb7659f5015c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475331} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bd6c4a2-af1f-5698-a7b3-9ffe19512167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474812} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9541a5-f46e-5e6b-afb1-b87a0a138a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474813} :answer-field/id #uuid "4675387e-3ac9-5537-9ed6-f89ff87766bb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475332} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f901af94-c98d-501a-b234-ab17178d0c8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474814} {:answer/id #uuid "336c0586-f08c-5ea5-a8bb-a690031bc9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474815}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186474814} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe925f85-a920-529c-848e-b9bb308c481f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186475333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8f1ed82d-67ec-55b7-826d-30733bc2c88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-35575" :question/problem "Which of the following sets is a subspace of ${R}^{2}?$\n\nI. $A=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=0\\}$\n\nII. $B=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=3x\\}$\n\nIII. $C=\\{⟨x,y⟩\\in {R}^{2}:y=2\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=0$ since $0=0.$ So, $0$ lies in $A.$\n\n- The set $B$ is a subspace. It represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. Here, the components of the zero vector $0=⟨0,0⟩$ satisfy the equation $y=3x$ since $0=3\\cdot 0.$ So, $0$ lies in $B.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $0=⟨0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=2$ since $0\\ne 2.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "353deabb-99c1-527a-9354-09b539a6e8de" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434597 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26487427-5f9b-54b2-bf84-c50ca7d67c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467238} {:answer/id #uuid "8abe74c0-b916-592e-8e38-169447a7be01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467239}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467238} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bb369ea-eb84-5999-9ef8-b0df4c5228f7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468340} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f749e06-8ca2-52aa-9e98-1b67c0b6a908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467240} {:answer/id #uuid "c92f06ac-0ec3-5893-aea1-dd43200715b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467241}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467241} :answer-field/id #uuid "dffd8e82-1715-566d-a74e-b5ca9f8ee2b6" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468341} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21aeab67-d310-5a4e-9331-241f8caf4bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467242} {:answer/id #uuid "6101f1aa-f296-59f7-8d09-2eac34095f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467243}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186467242} :answer-field/id #uuid "56cc4e03-77d2-5272-af50-419f510cef6e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "113a385d-0512-508f-a50c-e45bc26c3c1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-34300" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccb3b1dc1f175f6f018cb10e47db43a2ac5b4377c565dec4ee389b2a80455203.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "63f6444a-ffbe-508f-8985-460e2401c922" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434596}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275442 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faf7a4cf-3f25-5b8f-bde4-9923876d7825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464546} {:answer/id #uuid "a86f0c43-aee7-5ff2-a86c-84da3a7b1ad3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464546} :answer-field/id #uuid "33bd9e4a-04b7-5c12-a066-60b08ea5d84e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465207} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f78aa6be-523f-577b-b523-d2ef2b4c05ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464548} {:answer/id #uuid "c79bc708-2044-5eb0-bfdc-17e7b1fe97f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464549}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464548} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d0f943-2de6-55b4-ac77-581470c9653e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465208} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7dd21405-73dd-5db0-8d80-a73b2f2eef20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464550} {:answer/id #uuid "99c35460-0f76-558c-9d37-29b7c13ac41b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464551}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186464551} :answer-field/id #uuid "39f02546-0a6c-5523-907e-e1f1ba2cc467" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ce01e6e-4d84-5a03-940c-8de7146f3c76" :question/math-academy-id "q-158101" :question/problem "![](images/27/2739a0225640ff20cc9678edf8c41ba45ff637b17a40d0322ca792bef1346f06.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "769529b7-074a-511d-8739-3df09643b9d7"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ba35c72-4811-55d0-b2b1-fb8116a2cc3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461653} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2a422a-84a1-5671-bc55-3b45224e58cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461653} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0888cfa-5fff-5bcc-9c6f-d3cf0a398b99" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462580} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2974aa62-0b1f-5343-a4a5-c39a1584c078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218646165